{{- /* The git signing *public* key, as a bare `ssh-ed25519 AAAA…` string.

       Proton Pass is the source of truth: the key is a field of the `git
       signing key` item, the same item the private half comes from. Nothing
       here is secret — a public key is public — but keeping it out of the repo
       means one place to change when the key rotates instead of three.

       Two sources, in order, because neither is available everywhere:

       1. Proton Pass, gated on has-proton-session. The gate is not optional:
          chezmoi's protonPass function aborts the entire template when pass-cli
          fails, so calling it unguarded would break `chezmoi apply` in every
          container (no pass-cli) and in CI (no session).

       2. The ssh-agent, which holds the key whenever proton-ssh-load has run —
          and inside a container, where DevPod forwards the host's agent. Its
          `ssh-add -L` output is already in the right format. Wrapped in `sh -c`
          for the same reason as above: a missing agent must yield an empty
          string, not abort the template.

       If both fail this renders empty and dot_config/git/config.tmpl omits
       user.signingkey, which makes the next commit fail with "user.signingkey
       needs to be set" rather than silently producing unsigned commits. That is
       the intended behaviour: loud, and fixed by running proton-ssh-load. */ -}}
{{- if eq (includeTemplate "has-proton-session" .) "true" -}}
{{- protonPass "pass://HKQ0vUtADZgZYVg9bUWhhCWiTtGr3BN2EthtEvJIc_odo0SO-m8j2pQ3sHDqQqZ3XMo6q5E6KuybcW0LmLbm8Q==/oeoBEx8LHV1-NOk3sJuaYe56ocAn1DWxArqQKMYHdWqkkHm31pV4ZYCU2prvp-jdiVD_5oAOBBR8F4kE1BfoDA==/public_key" | trim -}}
{{- else -}}
{{- output "sh" "-c" "ssh-add -L 2>/dev/null | awk '/git-signing/{print $1\" \"$2; exit}'" | trim -}}
{{- end -}}
